<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonardo da Vinci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1452–<a href="/years/1519">1519</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonardo da Vinci was an Italian painter, engineer and scientist of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, born at Vinci near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, where he served Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> from the early 1480s, he painted the Last Supper; his other works include the Mona Lisa and notebooks of studies in anatomy, mechanics and nature. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci.jpg" alt="Leonardo da Vinci" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Francesco Melzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Melzi_-_Portrait_of_Leonardo_(colour_correction).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludovico Sforza’s court employed <b>Leonardo da Vinci</b> from about 1482 to 1499; there he painted the Last Supper in the refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, c. 1495–1498, designed a colossal equestrian monument to Francesco Sforza and filled notebooks with engineering and anatomical studies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the family’s Garden Palace and City Palace in Vienna; sales from it after the Second World War, when the family lost its estates in Czechoslovakia, helped sustain the dynasty, and <b>Leonardo da Vinci</b>’s Ginevra de’ Benci was sold to the National Gallery of Art in Washington in 1967.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…century Filippo Brunelleschi’s dome of Florence Cathedral, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of Donatello and the painting of Masaccio launched the artistic Renaissance, followed by Sandro Botticelli, <b>Leonardo da Vinci</b> and Michelangelo, whose David was set up outside the Palazzo Vecchio in 1504.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Anchiano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Clos Lucé, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.8,10.94&amp;year=1495" aria-label="Anchiano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4813 612 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leonardo da Vinci"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 882h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 882h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.8,10.94&amp;year=1495">What was here in 1495? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As a day well spent procures a happy sleep, so a life well employed procures a happy death.&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (philosophical maxims) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The water you touch in a river is the last of that which has passed, and the first of that which is coming. Thus it is with time present.&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (philosophical maxims) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Just as iron rusts unless it is used, and water putrifies or, in cold, turns to ice, so our intellect spoils unless it is kept in use.&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (philosophical maxims) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Experience never errs; it is only your judgments that err by promising themselves effects such as are not caused by your experiments.&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (philosophical maxims) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if you call painting dumb poetry, the painter may call poetry blind painting. Now which is the worse defect? to be blind or dumb?&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (the comparison of the arts) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no certainty in sciences where one of the mathematical sciences cannot be applied, or which are not in relation with these mathematics.&#8221;</p><footer>Notebooks (philosophical maxims) &#183; tr. Jean Paul Richter, 1883 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5000/pg5000-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
